<label>c</label>Krembil Research Institute, University Health Network, <city>Toronto</city>, <state>ON</state>, <country>Canada</country></aff><aff id="aff4">
<label>d</label>Faculty of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences, University of Melbourne, <city>Melbourne</city>, <state>VIC</state>, <country>Australia</country></aff><aff id="aff5">
<label>e</label>Department of Medical Biophysics, Temerty Faculty of Medicine, University of Toronto, <city>Toronto</city>, <state>ON</state>, <country>Canada</country></aff><aff id="aff6">
<label>f</label>Medical Engineering, University Health Network, <city>Toronto</city>, <state>ON</state>, <country>Canada</country></aff><aff id="aff7">
<label>g</label>Physical Sciences Platform, Sunnybrook Research Institute, Sunnybrook Health Sciences Centre, <city>Toronto</city>, <state>ON</state>, <country>Canada</country></aff><aff id="aff8">
<label>h</label>Institute of Medical Sciences, Temerty Faculty of Medicine, University of Toronto, <city>Toronto</city>, <state>ON</state>, <country>Canada</country></aff><aff id="aff9">
<label>i</label>Department of Neurosurgery, Royal Brisbane and Women's Hospital, <city>Brisbane</city>, <state>QLD</state>, <country>Australia</country></aff></contrib-group><author-notes><corresp id="cor1">Correspondence to: Alexandre Boutet, <email>alexandre.boutet@uhn.ca</email></corresp><fn fn-type="other"><p>Institution contact information: Toronto Western Hospital Medical Imaging Department Division of Neuroradiology 399 Bathurst Street Toronto, ON, M5T 2S8.</p></fn></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>27</day><month>11</month><year>2024</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>2</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><volume>103</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>42</fpage><lpage>54</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>15</day><month>5</month><year>2024</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>19</day><month>11</month><year>2024</year></date><date date-type="subscription-year"><year>2025</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>&#x000a9; 2024 The Author(s). Published by S. Karger AG, Basel</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2024</copyright-year><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbynclicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This article is licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License (CC BY) (<ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.karger.com/Services/OpenAccessLicense">http://www.karger.com/Services/OpenAccessLicense</ext-link>). Usage, derivative works and distribution are permitted provided that proper credit is given to the author and the original publisher.</license-p></license></permissions><abstract><title>Abstract</title><sec><title>Introduction</title><p>Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is both a crucial clinical and research tool for patients with deep brain stimulation (DBS) devices. However, safety concerns predominantly related to device heating have limited such imaging. Rigorous safety testing has demonstrated that scanning outside of vendor guidelines may be both safe and feasible, unlocking unique opportunities for advanced imaging in this patient population. Currently, however, 3T MRI safety data including advanced MRI sequences in novel directional and sensing DBS devices is lacking.</p></sec><sec><title>Methods</title><p>An anthropomorphic phantom replicating bilateral DBS system was used to assess the temperature rise at the electrode tips, implantable pulse generator, and cranial loop during acquisition of routine clinical sequences (three dimensional [3D] T1, GRE T2*, T2 FSE) and advanced imaging sequences including functional MRI (fMRI), arterial spin labelling (ASL), and diffusion weighted imaging (DWI). Measures of radiofrequency exposure (specific absorption rate [SAR] and root-mean square value of the MRI effective component of the radiofrequency transmission field [B<sub>1+rms</sub>]) were also recorded as an indirect measure of heating. Testing involved both a new directional and sensing DBS device (Medtronic: B30015 leads and Percept PC neurostimulator) and a previous-generation DBS device (Medtronic: 3,387 leads and Percept PC neurostimulator) in combination with a state-of-the-art (Siemens MAGNETOM Prisma) and a previous-generation (GE Signa HDxt) 3T MRI scanner.</p></sec><sec><title>Results</title><p>On the state-of-the-art 3T MRI scanner, the new DBS device produced safe temperature rises with clinically used sequences and fMRI but not with other advanced sequences such as DWI and ASL, which also exceeded B<sub>1+rms</sub> vendor guidelines (i.e., &#x02264;2 &#x003bc;T). When scanned on the previous MRI scanner, the recent DBS device produced overall lower and slower temperature rises compared to the previous DBS model. Among the sequences performed on this scanner, several (3D T1, DWI, T2 FSE, and ASL) exceeded the approved SAR vendor limit (&#x0003c;1 W/kg), but only ASL resulted in an unacceptable temperature rise during scanning of the previous DBS model.</p></sec><sec><title>Conclusion</title><p>These phantom safety data show that both clinically used MRI sequences and research sequences such as fMRI can be successfully acquired on 3T MRI scanners with a novel directional and sensing DBS model. As several of these sequences were obtained outside regulatory-approved vendor guidelines, preemptive safety testing should be done. As directional leads become increasingly common, improving MRI safety knowledge is crucial to expand clinical and research possibilities.</p></sec></abstract><abstract abstract-type="plain-language-summary"><title>Plain Language Summary</title><p>Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is a crucial clinical and research tool for patients with deep brain stimulation (DBS) devices, but MRI safety concerns, especially regarding device heating have limited its use. Rigorous safety testing has demonstrated that MRI outside of approved vendor guidelines for DBS may be both safe and feasible, unlocking new research opportunities. However, safety data for 3T MRI with newer directional and sensing DBS devices, including advanced sequences, are lacking. We conducted a phantom safety test with these new DBS systems to measure temperature during acquisition of routine clinical and advanced imaging sequences including functional MRI (fMRI). Testing compared a new directional and sensing DBS device and a previous-generation DBS device in combination with a state-of-the-art and a previous-generation 3T MRI scanner. Results show that routine and functional MRI sequences may be safely acquired in terms of temperature rise with a novel directional and sensing DBS device on both the state-of-the-art and the previous-generation MRI scanner at 3T, but some advances sequences may still pose risks. As they become increasingly common, understanding MRI safety of newer directional and sensing DBS device is crucial to expand their MRI possibilities.</p></abstract><kwd-group><title>Keywords</title><kwd>Deep brain stimulation</kwd><kwd>Magnetic resonance imaging</kwd><kwd>Safety testing</kwd><kwd>Functional MRI</kwd><kwd>Functional imaging</kwd></kwd-group><funding-group><funding-statement>This study was supported by the pilot fund of the Slaight Family Center for Advanced MRI: data acquisition.</funding-statement></funding-group><counts><fig-count count="4"/><table-count count="4"/><ref-count count="44"/><page-count count="13"/></counts></article-meta></front><body><sec sec-type="introduction" id="s1"><title>Introduction</title><p>Deep brain stimulation (DBS) is a well-established treatment for conditions such as Parkinson&#x02019;s disease (PD), essential tremor, and dystonia [<xref rid="B1" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="B3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>]. It is also currently under investigation as a potential therapy for psychiatric and cognitive disorders like major depressive disorder and Alzheimer&#x02019;s disease [<xref rid="B3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>]. A 2023 estimate indicated that over 244,000 patients have been implanted with DBS devices worldwide, with increasing procedures each year [<xref rid="B4" ref-type="bibr">4</xref>]. The majority of DBS patients will need a diagnostic MRI within a decade of their implantation [<xref rid="B5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref>]. MRI sequences &#x02013; especially advanced sequences such as functional MRI (fMRI) and diffusion weighted imaging (DWI) &#x02013; are also increasingly recognized as useful tools for investigating the mechanism of action of DBS [<xref rid="B6" ref-type="bibr">6</xref>] and optimizing this therapy [<xref rid="B7" ref-type="bibr">7</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="B9" ref-type="bibr">9</xref>]. However, in part due to serious past adverse events and highly restrictive vendor guidelines approved by healthcare regulatory agencies, patients with implanted DBS hardware are often denied MRI [<xref rid="B10" ref-type="bibr">10</xref>]. Recent safety testing suggests it may be possible to expand MRI usage in these patients [<xref rid="B11" ref-type="bibr">11</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="B13" ref-type="bibr">13</xref>]. Improving our safety knowledge of MRI in patients with DBS is particularly crucial as DBS systems equipped with directional steering and sensing capabilities have recently been introduced into clinical practice. They are set to become more widespread, but safety data at 3 tesla (T) magnetic field strength are currently lacking.</p><p>Safety concerns have driven the implementation of strict model-specific vendor guidelines for MRI scanning of DBS devices, predominantly intended to minimize the risk of tissue damage from heating [<xref rid="B14" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>]. During DBS surgery, metallic depth electrodes are placed in specific brain regions and connected via tunneled extension wires to an implantable pulse generator (IPG) that is typically positioned in the upper chest. It is well known that hardware components can couple with the radiofrequency (RF) fields produced by MRI, leading to heating during scanning [<xref rid="B15" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>]. Additionally, other parameters can contribute to electrode heating, such as gradient-induced heating [<xref rid="B16" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>], but their impact on electrode leads is typically minor due to the small wire cross-section. The RF exposure is measured as the specific absorption rate (SAR), which quantifies the rate at which energy is absorbed by the body tissue per unit mass during an MRI scan and the root-mean square value of the effective component of the B<sub>1</sub> field (B<sub>1+rms</sub>), which represents the average effective RF magnetic field generated by the RF transmit coil for a given pulse sequence. These parameters serve as a critical indicator of the potential for heating. The relationship between SAR and temperature rise depends on a complex interplay of several factors, including the specific device model and geometry of the device components [<xref rid="B1" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>, <xref rid="B14" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>], and the intensity and location of the RF fields generated by the MRI during imaging [<xref rid="B15" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>]. Mitigating heating becomes particularly important for MRI at 3T and higher magnetic fields, which are favored for their increased signal-to-noise ratio, contrast-to-noise ratio, and spatial resolution [<xref rid="B17" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>] but result in increased global and localized RF exposures. These exposures are linked to greater heating, partly due to a global increase in RF intensity (with SAR increasing quadratically with the main magnetic field strength). Considerable MRI physics and biomedical engineering work is being done to develop robust strategies to counter this complexity but at present, no revolutionary options are available.</p><p>To lower the risk of heating as much as possible, therefore, the vendor guidelines in the operating manuals of some DBS devices designate them in regulatory parlance as &#x0201c;MRI-conditional devices&#x0201d;: permitting some imaging capability while placing limits on certain parameters, including SAR and B<sub>1+rms</sub>, but also the main magnetic field intensities, coil configurations, and gradient modulation settings. In the past, many DBS devices have been designated as completely &#x0201c;MRI-unsafe,&#x0201d; but the number of MRI-conditional devices is increasing. Unfortunately, the imaging restrictions imposed on MRI-conditional DBS devices can be very stringent; in many cases, institutions seeking to scan DBS patients must extensively modify clinical MRI protocols, potentially lengthening scan times or requiring oversight from radiologists or MRI physicists in attempts to maintain (or mitigate reduction of) image quality [<xref rid="B14" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>]. Another response to these restrictions has been to perform scanner- and DBS hardware-specific safety testing to clear &#x0201c;off-label&#x0201d; MRI sequences or imaging parameter conditions for clinical and research use in DBS patients. To date, several such studies have been conducted using field strengths of 3 tesla and various off-label pulse sequences, leading to subsequent MRI acquisition without adverse events in over 150 patients [<xref rid="B1" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>, <xref rid="B11" ref-type="bibr">11</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="B13" ref-type="bibr">13</xref>, <xref rid="B18" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>]. These studies have opened the door to acquiring advanced MRI sequences with state-of-the-art parameters in this unique patient cohort. Crucially, although select newer DBS devices are 3T conditional, current guidelines significantly limit which sequences can be performed. It remains unclear which clinical and research sequences may be safely acquired.</p><p>Although MRI in DBS patients is usually acquired to rule out postoperative structural abnormalities [<xref rid="B19" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>], advanced imaging techniques such as fMRI have greatly contributed to our understanding of how DBS affects brain circuits [<xref rid="B20" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>, <xref rid="B21" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>]. Recent data have shown that the fMRI signal is related to clinical outcomes in PD [<xref rid="B21" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>], dystonia [<xref rid="B22" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>], chronic pain [<xref rid="B23" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>], and depression [<xref rid="B24" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>, <xref rid="B25" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>], raising the prospect that this technique could help guide the process of postoperative DBS stimulation parameter selection (i.e., &#x0201c;programming&#x0201d;). At present, this programming process is largely conducted by trial and error and relies on time-consuming clinical observations and clinician expertise [<xref rid="B26" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>]. By pairing advanced imaging data with machine learning techniques [<xref rid="B21" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>], it may be possible to derive robust, objective biomarkers of clinically optimal stimulation. Such markers could expedite DBS programming for indications such as PD and bring about even more dramatic benefits for conditions such as dystonia and depression, in which clinical response to stimulation changes is typically delayed.</p><p>Here, we assess the safety of 3T MRI with a recent directional and sensing DBS device, employing a variety of pulse sequences, including fMRI. The selection of 3T MRI for this evaluation is strategic, aligning with its widespread adoption in clinical and research settings. We specifically investigated standard clinical and advanced MRI sequences on a recent directional and sensing DBS device (Medtronic: B30015 leads and Percept PC neurostimulator) using a state-of-the-art MRI scanner (Siemens MAGNETOM Prisma). Following this, we compared this recent DBS device (Medtronic: B30015 leads and Percept PC neurostimulator) with a previous-generation DBS device (Medtronic: 3,387 leads and Percept PC neurostimulator) when scanned on a previous-generation 3T scanner (GE Signa HDxt). Characterizing the interactions between DBS devices and the specific configurations of 3T MRI units from different manufacturers is important since significant differences in heating effects can occur, even for identical device and phantom setups.</p></sec><sec sec-type="methods" id="s2"><title>Methods</title><sec id="s2-1"><title>Phantom Setup and Experimental Configuration</title><p>All testing was performed using an anthropomorphic three-dimensional (3D)-printed phantom with bilateral DBS device (shown in <xref rid="F1" ref-type="fig">Fig. 1</xref>, <xref rid="F2" ref-type="fig">2</xref>), as previously described [<xref rid="B27" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>]. Both the skull, and the assembled phantom including the skull were filled with a gel solution conforming to ASTM standards, resulting in a conductivity of approximately 0.47 S/m and a relative permittivity of approximately 80 [<xref rid="B28" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>]. To best replicate the conventional positioning of a DBS patient undergoing MRI, the phantom was positioned headfirst in a supine orientation and then landmarked at a location typical for brain imaging. Similar to stimulation settings routinely utilized in PD patients, the IPG was turned on with monopolar stimulation at the most distal contact with 3.0 mA current, 60 &#x000b5;s pulse width, and 130 Hz frequency using cycling mode [<xref rid="B21" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>].</p><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Fig. 1.</label><caption><p>Anthropomorphic phantom model. The anthropomorphic phantom used in our safety experiments is shown. The phantom was positioned supine with a headfirst orientation, mirroring real-life scenarios and conforming with conventional patient positioning protocols for brain imaging. In terms of DBS hardware, the phantom incorporated two electrode leads within the skull (visible in <xref rid="F2" ref-type="fig">Fig. 2</xref>) as well as extension wires (<bold>a</bold>) that connected these leads to the IPG (<bold>b</bold>). The same phantom configuration was utilized across scenarios 1, 2, and 3. Refer to <xref rid="T1" ref-type="table">Table 1</xref> for scenario specifications. DBS, deep brain stimulation; IPG, implantable pulse generator.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="sfn-2025-0103-0001-542725_F01" position="float"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>Fig. 2.</label><caption><p>Positioning of lead trajectories in the phantom brain. A cross-section of the skull from the anthropomorphic phantom used in our safety experiments is depicted. The figure illustrates how DBS electrode lead trajectories within the skull were achieved. DBS, deep brain stimulation.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="sfn-2025-0103-0001-542725_F02" position="float"/></fig><p>Three different scenarios are tested, summarized in <xref rid="T1" ref-type="table">Table 1</xref>.<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Scenario 1: recent directional and sensing DBS device (Model B30015, Medtronic, Inc.); state-of-the-art 3T MRI scanner (Siemens MAGNETOM Prisma 3.0 T, Siemens AG);</p></list-item><list-item><p>Scenario 2: recent directional and sensing DBS device (Model B30015, Medtronic, Inc.); previous-generation 3T MRI scanner (GE SIGNA HDxt 3.0 T, GE Healthcare);</p></list-item><list-item><p>Scenario 3: previous-generation DBS device (Model 3387, Medtronic, Inc.); previous-generation 3T MRI scanner (GE SIGNA HDxt 3.0 T, GE Healthcare).</p></list-item></list></p><table-wrap position="float" id="T1"><label>Table 1.</label><caption><p>Summary of MRI scanner and DBS device configurations tested with phantom experiments</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Scenario</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Scanner</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Scanner model</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">DBS device</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">DBS leads</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">IPG</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Extensions</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">State-of-the-art 3T MRI scanner</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Siemens MAGNETOM Prisma 3.0T, Siemens AG</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Recent directional and sensing</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2 &#x000d7; B30015 (Medtronic, Inc.)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Percept PC B35200 (Medtronic, Inc.)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2 &#x000d7; B34000, 60 cm (Medtronic, Inc.)</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Previous-generation 3T MRI scanner</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">GE SIGNA HDxt 3.0T, GE Healthcare</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Recent directional and sensing</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2 &#x000d7; B30015 (Medtronic, Inc.)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Percept PC B35200 (Medtronic, Inc.)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2 &#x000d7; B34000, 60 cm (Medtronic, Inc.)</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Previous-generation 3T MRI scanner</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">GE SIGNA HDxt 3.0T, GE Healthcare</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Previous-generation</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2 &#x000d7; 3,387 (Medtronic, Inc.)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Percept PC B35200 (Medtronic, Inc.)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2 &#x000d7; 7,482 (Medtronic, Inc.)</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>DBS, deep brain stimulation; IPG, implantable pulse generator; MRI, magnetic resonance imaging.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><p>The experimental DBS setup was completely redone for each of the three scenarios. A transmit-body coil and receive-only head coil were used for MRI in all three scenarios: an integrated 2-channel transmit-body was used each time (standard on each MRI system, with vendor-specific and generation-specific differences); whereas scenario 1 used a 32-channel receive-only head coils and scenarios 2 and 3 used an 8-channel receive-only head coil. The transmit-body coil was used for optimal image quality although it increases DBS device RF exposure resulting in increased device heating [<xref rid="B29" ref-type="bibr">29</xref>]. The transmit-body coil on the new MRI scanner included parallel RF transmission (PTX) capability for improved image uniformity, whereas the analogous coil on the previous MRI scanner did not. Multiple routinely acquired and advanced MRI sequences (DWI, arterial spin labelling [ASL], and fMRI) were tested in each scenario (see <xref rid="T2" ref-type="table">Tables 2</xref>, <xref rid="T3" ref-type="table">3</xref> for acquisition parameters). Since our goal was to use sequences optimized for each scanner, thus mimicking real-life scenarios, the acquisition parameters were different for both 3T MRI units. The state-of-the-art scanner has shorter repetition time values, stronger gradients, and different RF optimization compared to the previous scanner. In addition, the scans on the start-of-the-art scanner that had T2 contrast-weighting were acquired in 3D acquisition, whereas on the previous scanner they were acquired as a two-dimensional acquisition.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="T2"><label>Table 2.</label><caption><p>Pulse sequence acquisition parameters for scenario 1</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Image pulse sequence</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">3D T1</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">(2D) GRE T2*</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">BOLD (fMRI)</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">(2D SMS-EPI) DWI (64 directions)</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">T2 FSE</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">(3D) ASL</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">(2D) WAIR</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">TR, ms</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2,100</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">933</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2,190</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3,700</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3,200</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">4,200</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">5,520</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">TE, ms</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.96</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.75 (TE1) 7.50 (TE2)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">30</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">86</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">409</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">19.4</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">16</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">TI, ms</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">814</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NP</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NP</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NP</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NP</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NP</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">450</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">BW, Hz/px</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">240</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">600</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1,184</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1,488</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">751</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2,104</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">114</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Echo spacing, ms</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">7.08</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NP</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.93</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.76</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.62</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NP</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">15.5</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">FOV, mm</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">256</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">192</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">240</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">230</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">256</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">220</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">218</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">FA, &#x000b0;</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">8</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">60</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">76</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NP</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NP</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">180</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">120</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Number of slices</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">224</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">90</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">40</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">68</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">240</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">58</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">54</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">ST, mm</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.5</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.5</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Phase-encoding direction</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Anterior&#x0003e;&#x0003e;posterior</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Posterior&#x0003e;&#x0003e;anterior</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Anterior&#x0003e;&#x0003e;posterior</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Anterior&#x0003e;&#x0003e;posterior</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Right&#x0003e;&#x0003e;left</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Posterior&#x0003e;&#x0003e;anterior</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Right&#x0003e;&#x0003e;left</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">TA</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3 min 55 s</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 min 42 s</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">6 min 45 s</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">8 min 17 s</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3 min 04 s</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">5 min 56 s</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">6 min 28 s</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>2D, two dimensional; 3D, three dimensional; A, anterior; ASL, arterial spin labeling; BOLD, blood oxygenation level dependent; BW, bandwidth; DWI, diffusion weighted imaging; EPI, echo planar imaging; ETL, echo train length; fMRI, functional magnetic resonance imaging; FA, flip angle; FSE, fast spin echo; FOV, field of view; GRE, gradient echo; NP, not provided; SMS, simultaneous multislice; ST, slice thickness; TA, acquisition time; TE, echo time; TE1, echo time 1; TE2, echo time 2; TI, inversion time; TR, repetition time; WAIR, white matter attenuated inversion recovery.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap position="float" id="T3"><label>Table 3.</label><caption><p>Pulse sequence acquisition parameters for scenario 2 and 3</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Image pulse sequence</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">3D T1</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">(2D) GRE T2*</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">BOLD (fMRI)</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">DWI (6 directions)</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">T2 FSE</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">(3D) ASL</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">TR, ms</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">210</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">985</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2,151</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">12,400</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">9,384</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NP</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">TE, ms</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Min</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">25</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">30</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Min</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">102</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NP</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">TI, ms</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">450</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NP</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NP</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NP</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NP</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NP</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">BW, kHz</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">31.25</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">31.25</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NP</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NP</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">25</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">62.5</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">FOV, cm</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">25.6</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">24</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">24</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">24</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">24</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">22</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">FA, &#x000b0;</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">20</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">15</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">76</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">180</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">180</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NP</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Number of slices</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">170</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">32</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">36</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">46</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">48</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">46</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">ST, mm</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">5</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">4</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">4</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">ETL</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NP</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NP</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NP</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NP</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">12</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NP</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Matrix</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">256 &#x000d7; 256</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">256 &#x000d7; 256</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">64 &#x000d7; 64</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">128 &#x000d7; 128</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">256 &#x000d7; 224</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">512 &#x000d7; 8</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Frequency-encoding direction</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Anterior/posterior</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Anterior/posterior</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Right/left</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Right/left</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Anterior/posterior</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Anterior/posterior</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NEX</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">NP</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">TA</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">4 min 47 s</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">6 min 22 s</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">6 min 30 s</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">7 min 2 s</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3 min 17 s</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">5 min 14 s</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>2D, two dimensional; 3D, three dimensional; ASL, arterial spin labeling; BOLD, blood oxygenation level dependent; BW, bandwidth; DWI, diffusion weighted imaging; ETL, echo train length; FA, flip angle; FOV, field of view; FSE, fast spin echo; GRE, gradient echo; Min, minimum; NEX, number of excitations; NP, not provided; ST, slice thickness; TA, acquisition time; TE, echo time; TI, inversion time; TR, repetition time; WAIR, white matter attenuated inversion recovery.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></sec><sec id="s2-2"><title>Temperature Monitoring</title><p>As in previous work [<xref rid="B27" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>], temperature changes associated with MRI were measured using 4 fiber-optic temperature sensors linked to a fiber-optic thermometer (T1, Neoptix, Quebec City, QB, Canada). The sensors have a resolution of &#x000b1; 0.2&#x000b0;C. Two sensors were positioned at the most distal contacts of each DBS lead, which is the location at which the highest level of heating is suspected to occur [<xref rid="B14" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>]. A third temperature sensor was positioned within the looped excess extension wire near the vertex at the right posterolateral area, while a fourth was placed at the posterior aspect of the IPG. A conservative approach was used where the maximum temperature change for each pulse sequence was defined as the difference between the maximal temperature recorded during sequence acquisition and the baseline temperature recorded at the start of each sequence. A temperature increase of less than 2&#x000b0;C was deemed acceptable providing a sufficient margin of safety [<xref rid="B30" ref-type="bibr">30</xref>]. US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) regulations state that a device heating adjacent thermally sensitive tissues up to 2&#x000b0;C in 15 min in Normal Operating Mode can be scanned for 1 h without a cooling period [<xref rid="B31" ref-type="bibr">31</xref>]. In addition to temperature changes, we also tracked indirect measures of heating included in vendor guidelines: head-averaged SAR and B<sub>1+rms</sub>. Head-averaged SAR values were recorded directly from the scanner console during imaging in all scenarios. The correlation between head SAR and temperature rise at the electrode tips was determined using linear regression analysis. The coefficient of determination (<italic toggle="yes">R</italic><sup>2</sup>) was calculated for each scenario to quantify the strength of the relationship between SAR values and the maximal temperature increases observed at the right and left electrode tips. Statistical analyses were conducted using IBM SPSS Statistics software (IBM Corp. Released 2020. IBM SPSS Statistics for Windows, Version 27.0. Armonk, NY: IBM Corp). For B<sub>1+rms</sub>, data were recorded from the state-of-the-art 3T MRI scanner during imaging in scenario 1 but were not available from the previous 3T MRI scanner in scenarios 2 and 3, as the B<sub>1+rms</sub> parameter has been incorporated by regulatory agencies relatively recently. As per Medtronic guidelines for the recent directional and sensing DBS device, the B<sub>1+rms</sub> should not exceed 2.0 &#x003bc;T at 3T [<xref rid="B32" ref-type="bibr">32</xref>]. In cases where 3T MRI scanners do not provide the user a B<sub>1+rms</sub> value, Medtronic guidelines prescribe maintaining SAR below 1.0W/kg [<xref rid="B32" ref-type="bibr">32</xref>]. No attempt was made at directly comparing absolute temperature rise between the two scanners. As mentioned above, the goal was specifically to evaluate anthropomorphic phantom-based approximations of real-life scenarios where each DBS device was used under routine, optimized MRI conditions on each scanner.</p></sec></sec><sec sec-type="results" id="s3"><title>Results</title><p>
<xref rid="T4" ref-type="table">Table 4</xref> shows the maximal temperature rises, head SAR values, and B<sub>1+rms</sub> values for each scenario. For the recent directional and sensing DBS device and the state-of-the-art MRI scanner (scenario 1), most tested pulse sequences (including fMRI but excluding DWI, WAIR, and ASL) demonstrated temperature rises below 2&#x000b0;C over the duration of the scan. When scanned on the previous MRI scanner, the recent directional and sensing DBS device produced less temperature rise compared to the previous DBS model, with all tested pulse sequences causing temperature rises below 2&#x000b0;C. Acknowledging differences in sequence parameters due to different technical capabilities, the previous MRI scanner (scenarios 2 and 3) resulted overall in lower and slower temperature increases for the same amount of head SAR compared with the state-of-the-art MRI unit (shown in <xref rid="F3" ref-type="fig">Fig. 3</xref>). Notably, all sequences tested on the previous scanner triggered a temperature rise below 2&#x000b0;C, except for ASL (<xref rid="T4" ref-type="table">Table 4</xref>).</p><table-wrap position="float" id="T4"><label>Table 4.</label><caption><p>Summary of the observed maximal temperature rises and the associated head-averaged SAR and B<sub>1+rms</sub></p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th rowspan="2" colspan="1">Sequence</th><th colspan="4" rowspan="1">Scenario 1</th><th colspan="3" rowspan="1">Scenario 2</th><th colspan="3" rowspan="1">Scenario 3</th></tr><tr><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">head SAR, W/kg</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">B1+ rms, &#x003bc;T</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Max &#x00394;T, &#x000b0;C</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">position</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">head SAR, W/kg</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Max &#x00394;T, &#x000b0;C</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">position</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">head SAR, W/kg</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Max &#x00394;T, &#x000b0;C</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">position</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3D T1</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.15</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.84</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Right electrode tip</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.36</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.4</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Left electrode tip</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.36</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.9</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Right electrode tip</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">GRE T2*</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.33</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.23</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.7</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Right electrode tip</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.14</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.4</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Right electrode tip</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.14</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.3</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Cranial loop</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">BOLD</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.95</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.5</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Right electrode tip</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.91</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.3</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Right electrode tip</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.91</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.7</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Right electrode tip</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">DWI</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.15</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.27</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Right electrode tip</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.61</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.6</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Left electrode tip</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.61</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.5</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Right electrode tip</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">T2 FSE</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.35</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.52</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.6</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Right electrode tip</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.18</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.7</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Left electrode tip</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.18</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.3</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Right electrode tip</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">ASL</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.54</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.4</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.6</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Left electrode tip</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.7</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Left electrode tip</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.2</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.3</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Right electrode tip</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">WAIR</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.88</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.98</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.4</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Right electrode tip</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>Only maximal temperature rises across the 4 thermometer probes are reported.</p></fn><fn><p>3D, three dimensional; ASL, arterial spin labeling; B1+rms, root mean square value of the radiofrequency magnetic field (B1); BOLD, blood oxygenation level dependent; DWI, diffusion weighted imaging; FSE, fast spin echo; GRE, gradient echo; IPG, implantable pulse generator; Max &#x02206;T, maximal temperature rise SAR, specific absorption rate; WAIR, white matter attenuated inversion recovery.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>Fig. 3.</label><caption><p>Relationships between temperature rise and SAR. The maximal change in temperature (Max &#x02206;Temperature) is plotted against the head-averaged SAR (head SAR) for the right (<bold>a</bold>) and left (<bold>b</bold>) electrode tips for the different sequences acquired in each scenario. The red line indicates the accepted safe threshold of 2&#x000b0;C of heating. The blue (scenario 1), orange (scenario 2) and green (scenario 3) lines represent linear fits to the respective colored experimental data points. Note that the linear models assumed an intercept at 0. Uncertainty bars of &#x000b1; 0.2&#x000b0;C represent the resolution of the fiber-optic temperature sensors. SAR, specific absorption rate.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="sfn-2025-0103-0001-542725_F03" position="float"/></fig><p>Generally, the highest temperature rise was recorded at the electrode tips. Interestingly, the highest temperature rises were noted on the right or the left depending on the scenarios. While scanning the recent directional and sensing DBS device with the state-of-the-art MRI scanner (scenario 1), the maximal temperature rise was most commonly recorded at the right electrode tip. Conversely, for the recent directional and sensing DBS device on the previous MRI scanner (scenario 2), the left electrode tip typically exhibited the highest temperature increase. For the previous DBS device on the previous MRI scanner (scenario 3), the highest temperature rise was recorded mostly at the right electrode tip. Temperature rise dynamics of a representative sequence (ASL) at the left electrode tip are illustrated in <xref rid="F4" ref-type="fig">Figure 4</xref>. The temperature increases recorded at the sensor located at the vertex of the phantom model and at the posterior aspect of the IPG were always below 2&#x000b0;C across the tested scenarios (see online suppl. Tables 1, 2; for all online suppl. material, see <ext-link xlink:href="10.1159/000542725" ext-link-type="doi">https://doi.org/10.1159/000542725</ext-link>).</p><fig position="float" id="F4"><label>Fig. 4.</label><caption><p>Changes in temperature at the left electrode tip during ASL MRI in scenarios 1, 2, and 3. The recorded change in temperature (&#x02206;T) for each scenario is plotted over time (scenario 1, colored blue line; scenario 2, colored orange line; scenario 3, colored green line), starting from the beginning of the pulse sequence. The end of the sequence in each scenario is indicated by a dot of the appropriate color, respectively. Acknowledging that the sequence acquisition parameters were not identical on both MRI scanners, our goal was to replicate real-life conditions as closely as possible using optimized sequence parameters for both scanners. ASL, arterial spin labelling.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="sfn-2025-0103-0001-542725_F04" position="float"/></fig><p>Many of the tested sequences that triggered an acceptable rise in temperature were also within the approved range of indirect measures of heating (i.e., SAR and B<sub>1+rms</sub>). For the recent directional and sensing DBS device and the state-of-the-art MRI scanners (scenario 1), all sequences fell within the approved B<sub>1+rms</sub> limit from the vendor (i.e., &#x02264;2 &#x003bc;T), except for DWI and ASL, which also produced temperature increases &#x0003e;2&#x000b0;C. On the previous MRI scanner, several sequences (3D T1, DWI, T2 FSE, and ASL) exceeded the approved SAR vendor limit (&#x0003c;1 W/kg) but resulted in a temperature increase below 2&#x000b0;C, except for ASL. Sequences on the previous scanner that were associated with the highest head-averaged SAR values were also those that caused the highest temperature rise (i.e., T2 FSE and ASL).</p><p>As expected, a linear relationship between the temperature rise and head-average SAR was observed (shown in <xref rid="F3" ref-type="fig">Fig. 3</xref>). Utilizing this linear relationship, we extrapolated head-average SAR thresholds for a 2&#x000b0;C temperature rise in the electrode heating the most in each experiment (shown in <xref rid="F3" ref-type="fig">Fig. 3</xref>): 1.04 W/Kg in scenario 1 (right electrode tip), 7.37 W/Kg in scenario 2 (left electrode tip), and 3.57 W/Kg in scenario 3 (right electrode tip). There was a significant relationship between head SAR and the maximal temperature rise in the majority of the scenarios (right electrode tip scenario 1: <italic toggle="yes">R</italic><sup>2</sup> = 0.61, <italic toggle="yes">p</italic> = 0.039; left electrode tip scenario 1: <italic toggle="yes">R</italic><sup>2</sup> = 0.99, <italic toggle="yes">p</italic> &#x0003c; 0.001; right electrode tip scenario 2: <italic toggle="yes">R</italic><sup>2</sup> = 0.61, <italic toggle="yes">p</italic> = 0.068; left electrode tip scenario 2: <italic toggle="yes">R</italic><sup>2</sup> = 0.85, <italic toggle="yes">p</italic> = 0.009; right electrode tip scenario 3: <italic toggle="yes">R</italic><sup>2</sup> = 0.76, <italic toggle="yes">p</italic> = 0.024; left electrode tip scenario 3: <italic toggle="yes">R</italic><sup>2</sup> = 0.55, <italic toggle="yes">p</italic> = 0.092).</p></sec><sec sec-type="discussion" id="s4"><title>Discussion</title><p>Our phantom experiments indicate that most clinically used MRI sequences and fMRI may be acquired safely in patients implanted with recent directional and sensing DBS devices, using the two-specific MRI scanners and scanner configurations tested in the study. However, as suggested in prior studies [<xref rid="B11" ref-type="bibr">11</xref>, <xref rid="B14" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>], sequences which require large-amplitude RF pulses such as ASL and DWI may trigger unacceptable rises in temperature. Results suggest that the new DBS device may be safer than the previous model in terms of temperature rise. Similar to previous studies [<xref rid="B11" ref-type="bibr">11</xref>, <xref rid="B29" ref-type="bibr">29</xref>], our findings indicate that it might be possible to exceed vendor guidelines while staying within safe temperature limits, potentially allowing for expanded MRI capabilities while ensuring safety. A high degree of caution is warranted with state-of-the-art MRI scanners which tend to trigger more rapid and higher temperature increases.</p><p>MRI-related heating of DBS devices depends on various factors. These include the intrinsic electrical properties of the specific device as well as how its components (i.e., electrode leads, extension wires, and IPG) are positioned relative to the RF transmit coil [<xref rid="B33" ref-type="bibr">33</xref>]. The field strength of the MRI scanner, type and geometry of RF coil, sequence parameters, and amount of RF energy delivered (measured by both SAR and B<sub>1+rms</sub>) also directly impact heating levels. Similar to previous studies [<xref rid="B34" ref-type="bibr">34</xref>, <xref rid="B35" ref-type="bibr">35</xref>], we observed a linear relationship between temperature rise and SAR. The recent directional and sensing DBS device scanned on the state-of-the-art MRI scanner triggered the highest temperature for a given SAR. This could be attributed to the difference in the sequence protocol used rather than by the characteristics of the scanner itself. State-of-the-art MRI imaging protocols can take advantage of PTX capabilities to improve the spatial uniformity of imaging signals within the head, which might lead to reaching the 2&#x000b0;C temperature limit more rapidly compared to legacy imaging protocols. On the state-of-the-art MRI scanner, the right electrode tip exhibited a higher &#x00394;T/head SAR than the left. Conversely, when scanned on the previous MRI scanner, the right electrode tip displayed significantly lower &#x00394;T/head SAR. Also, the recent directional DBS device demonstrated overall lower &#x00394;T/head SAR compared to the previous DBS device. Differences in lateralization of electrode heating can be attributed to several factors, such as inherent differences in the DBS hardware, coil and loop configuration inconsistencies (with loops acting as inductors in the magnetic field), the angulation of the DBS components relative to the RF transmit coil, as well as RF transmit coil design and whether or not PTX is adopted. At higher magnetic field strengths (3T and above), RF coupling to the DBS system can potentially cause more localized heating and perturb the distribution of the magnetic RF field throughout the phantom. This phenomenon can lead to asymmetry when comparing local heating to global SAR metrics and B<sub>1+rms</sub> dosimetric values, which are approximations of the average exposure across the full field of view. It is important to note that this asymmetry is an expected behavior and represents a real physical property of RF interactions between devices and the incident RF fields at these field strengths [<xref rid="B36" ref-type="bibr">36</xref>]. The weaker correlation between head SAR and temperature rise at the right electrode tip is likely due to the asymmetric added extension wire length coursing over the head for the electrode contralateral to the IPG and its different orientation to the magnetic field (B0), reducing sensitivity to RF excitations as per Faraday&#x02019;s Law. This is consistent with our previous findings [<xref rid="B11" ref-type="bibr">11</xref>, <xref rid="B29" ref-type="bibr">29</xref>], where differences between the right and left sides were noted. While heating on the right side was similar, factors such as tissue variability and sensor placement may have contributed to the observed differences. The complexity of these interrelated variables, including specific geometry and RF fields, makes it nearly impossible to isolate a single causative factor and predict heating behavior.</p><p>Despite the introduction of recent directional and sensing DBS devices with expanded vendor guidelines (e.g., whole-body eligible), some of the sequences, particularly advanced imaging (DWI and ASL), may still remain unsafe. To maximize safety, MRI acquisitions should adhere to vendor specifications, allowing institutional protocol adjustments including reducing slice count, echo numbers, or flip angles, and extending repetition time intervals [<xref rid="B37" ref-type="bibr">37</xref>]. As with any test in medicine, clinicians must weigh the risks and benefits associated with performing a test. If an MRI is performed outside of vendor guidelines to provide significant benefit for the patient, a priori local safety testing should be conducted to minimize associated risks as much as possible and to ensure a reliable evaluation of the safety of a specific DBS configuration on a particular MRI scanner [<xref rid="B11" ref-type="bibr">11</xref>].</p><p>MRI already plays a key role in the care of DBS patients and will likely have an even larger impact going forward. While only structural imaging is commonly acquired for surgical planning and postoperative assessment of electrode placement, fMRI is increasingly prominent in the research sphere. Early studies in DBS patients had shown that fMRI can be used to confirm circuit-specific engagement [<xref rid="B1" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>, <xref rid="B11" ref-type="bibr">11</xref>]. However, these studies were halted due to MRI-related adverse events and as a result, the introduction of strict vendor guidelines [<xref rid="B1" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>, <xref rid="B38" ref-type="bibr">38</xref>, <xref rid="B39" ref-type="bibr">39</xref>]. Recent data from phantom studies have demonstrated the feasibility and safety of performing 3T MRI in DBS patients under specific conditions [<xref rid="B40" ref-type="bibr">40</xref>]. The number of fMRI studies acquired in DBS patients has drastically increased in the last few years, providing valuable insights into how delivered stimulation impacts brain function [<xref rid="B40" ref-type="bibr">40</xref>]. A recent meta-analysis by Loh et al. [<xref rid="B40" ref-type="bibr">40</xref>] found that the stimulation of the subthalamic nucleus produced consistent BOLD changes within the cortico-basal ganglia-thalamo-cortical (CSTC) loop and cerebellum in patients with PD. Additionally, resting state fMRI studies have demonstrated increased thalamo-cortical connectivity with DBS [<xref rid="B30" ref-type="bibr">30</xref>, <xref rid="B40" ref-type="bibr">40</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="B42" ref-type="bibr">42</xref>]. The 3T fMRI work has also deepened our understanding of DBS for treatment-resistant depression, finding that low-frequency BOLD changes along the course of the cingulum bundle relate to clinical outcomes [<xref rid="B24" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>]. These insights may prove particularly useful considering recent decisions by the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) to provide breakthrough device designation for treatment of depression with DBS [<xref rid="B43" ref-type="bibr">43</xref>], offering a potential biomarker of successful stimulation with which to guide postoperative programming. Finally, fMRI has the potential to play a crucial role in the planning and targeting phases of DBS surgery. A recent study conducted by Sammartino et al. [<xref rid="B44" ref-type="bibr">44</xref>] has explored the feasibility of utilizing fMRI in the intraoperative environment with patients under general anesthesia to provide real-time feedback. This could help confirm accurate electrode insertion.</p><p>Several limitations should be acknowledged. First, as described in prior studies, phantom experiments do not model blood flow; they underestimate heat dispersion and the impact of temperature regulation. Consequently, they tend to overestimate heating, presenting a worst-case scenario, which is desirable when assessing safety. Another limitation is the difference in hardware and software capabilities which prevented similar acquisition parameters between the state-of-the-art and previous MRI scanners. Specifically, the state-of-the-art scanner employed additional RF pulses, such as during the DWI sequence, potentially resulting in higher observed temperature rises. Furthermore, B<sub>1+rms</sub> could not be computed on the previous MRI scanner. Considering the recent inclusion of B<sub>1+rms</sub> in DBS vendor guidelines, its computation would have been preferable. Importantly, our results are not directly applicable to other institutions, which should perform their own phantom safety experiments before conducting off-label scanning of DBS patients. Moreover, the current study&#x02019;s results were obtained using DBS devices from a single manufacturer, limiting their generalizability to hardware produced by other DBS vendors.</p><p>As the population of patients with implanted recent directional and sensing DBS devices continues to grow, there will likely be an increasing demand for MRI among these individuals. While adhering to vendor guidelines for MRI procedures is recommended, this can sometimes adversely affect patient care. It is crucial for clinicians to carefully assess the risks and benefits of MRI scanning on a case-by-case basis in these situations [<xref rid="B29" ref-type="bibr">29</xref>].</p></sec><sec sec-type="conclusions" id="s5"><title>Conclusions</title><p>Our phantom safety test indicated that a variety of MRI sequences &#x02013; both standard clinical and research-focused, such as fMRI &#x02013; may be acquired safely in terms of temperature rise using 3T MRI systems with recent directional and sensing DBS models. The possibility of acquiring fMRI in patients with directional DBS systems could enhance our understanding of how this stimulation impacts brain networks and how to achieve maximal clinical benefits. Future MRI safety work in DBS patients will build upon the expanding body of research and contribute to expanded MRI usage in this patient population.</p></sec><sec sec-type="statement of ethics" id="s6"><title>Statement of Ethics</title><p>An ethics statement was not required for this study type since no human or animal subjects or materials were used.</p></sec><sec sec-type="COI-statement" id="s7"><title>Conflict of Interest Statement</title><p>A.M.L. is a consultant to Abbott, Boston Scientific, Insightec, Medtronic and Functional Neuromodulation (Scientific Director). The other authors report no conflict of interest.</p></sec><sec sec-type="funding sources" id="s8"><title>Funding Sources</title><p>This study was supported by the pilot fund of the Slaight Family Center for Advanced MRI: data acquisition.</p></sec><sec sec-type="author contributions" id="s9"><title>Author Contributions</title><p>R.L.: conceptualization, data curation, formal analysis, investigation, methodology, validation, writing &#x02013; original draft, writing &#x02013; review and editing. C.T.C.: conceptualization, data curation, data acquisition, formal analysis, investigation, methodology, validation, and writing &#x02013; review and editing. S.K., B.Y., S.J.G., G.J.E., B.S., A.N., D.M., M.C., J.G., A.V., K.U., A.M.L., and I.C.: methodology, data curation, formal analysis, validation, and writing &#x02013; review and editing. A.B.: conceptualization, data curation, data acquisition, methodology, validation, writing &#x02013; review and editing, and supervision.</p></sec></body><back><sec sec-type="data-availability" id="s11"><title>Data Availability Statement</title><p>All data generated or analyzed during this study are included in this article and its supplementary material files. Further inquiries can be directed to the corresponding author.</p></sec><sec sec-type="supplementary-material" id="s17"><label>Supplementary Material</label><supplementary-material id="SD1" position="float" content-type="local-data"><media xlink:href="Supplementary_material-Suppl.1-s1.docx" id="d67e1587" position="anchor"/></supplementary-material></sec><ref-list><title>References</title><ref id="B1"><label>1.</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal">
